When she stitched, the cloth did not merely hang as a curtain. It showed visions: the first voyage of a ship that would never sink, the last kiss of a storm that would break into rainbows, the quiet moment when a child learned to count stars. People began to bring Liora their own fables—tales of courage, mercy, and longing—that she wove into tapestries that warmed homes on winter nights.

But the thread demanded a barter: for every vision shown, a small truth must be released back into the world—an old fear set free, a memory unforgotten. The town learned that stories, once woven, carry responsibility. They kept weaving anyway, until the loom’s song became the harbor’s heartbeat, and every inhabitant slept under a quilt of remembered miracles.
